  "citations": [
    {
      "role": "cite",
      "name": "habeas:coverage",
      "commentary": "This private compatibility module only declares the legacy resource and\n selector address shapes; it has no handwritten executable code. Its derived\n serialization and identity operations do not emit source-line measurements.\n The source-tool argument and delivery regressions in `agent/tests.rs`\n exercise these addresses through the legacy adapter. Supported path-based\n address construction is tested separately in `tests/outline_content.rs`."
    }
  ],
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The `habeas:coverage` citation on `crates/trust-but-verify/src/resource.rs` is on the file root, and its commentary is substantive and matches the source.\n\n- **No handwritten executable code.** The file contains only the `ResourceId` and `OutlineId` struct declarations with derived `Clone`, `Debug`, `PartialEq`, `Eq`, `Serialize` and `Deserialize`. Nothing in it should produce instrumented source-line measurements, so the file-level exception is the right kind.\n- **The module is private or hidden.** `lib.rs` declares `mod resource` when `habeas-integration` is off, and `pub mod resource` marked `#[doc(hidden)]` when it is on. The commentary's \"private compatibility module\" is close enough to that.\n- **The `agent/tests.rs` claim holds.** `agent/tests.rs` imports `crate::resource::*`. In `fn[source_tools_forward_arguments_and_present_discovery_and_read_results]`, `Provider` builds `ResourceId` values. The `observation` closure takes `&OutlineId` values. The test asserts the serialized JSON of the resource and node addresses, including `{\"resource\":…,\"selector\":…}`. It also asserts the selectors recorded by the observation callback. That exercises the derived serialization and identity through the legacy adapter. The source-reads, outline and discovery regression tests in the same file use these types too.\n- **The `tests/outline_content.rs` claim holds.** `tests/outline_content.rs` has `fn[addresses_reconstruct_without_reading_the_file]` and others. They cover path-based `OutlineId::new` and `OutlineId::root` construction, serde round-tripping, selector validation and equality. These use the supported `outline::OutlineId`, not the legacy type, as the commentary says.\n\nThe commentary gives reasons why the incomplete coverage is acceptable and agrees with the current source. It does not rely on difficulty alone. I found no gap.",
